\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Differential Nocuity.}
\citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish nocuous from innocuous ambiguity by stakeholder divergence. Our results extend this to LLM agents: all six types are nocuous ($+34.0$ pp), but type primarily determines severity (22:1 clause-vs.-model variance ratio). We propose \emph{Differential Nocuity}: the risk of a policy ambiguity depends on type, safety training, and disambiguation channel availability. The 41.3\% majority-convergent violation rate (\emph{systematic nocuity}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}) suggests shared failure modes across architectures that make errors invisible in agreement-based evaluation. The replication across seven models spanning six providers (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}) confirms this is a structural property of LLM-based agents, not an idiosyncrasy of particular architectures.

\paragraph{Why incompleteness resists safety training.}
The exploratory model$\times$type interaction (C4) suggests that safety training does not uniformly reduce ambiguity vulnerability. Incompleteness is universally dangerous across all seven tested models (46--62\%), driven by a consistent gap-filling mechanism (\S\ref{sec:analysis}) that bypasses safety-training intervention points. The agent never encounters a decision point that RLHF refusal mechanisms can intercept: there is no ``should I or shouldn't I?'' moment, only a gap filled with plausible defaults \citep{ferreira_good_enough_2002,ferreira_patson_2007}. Authorization scope, by contrast, involves an explicit permission boundary that directly engages harm-avoidance objectives, making it a \emph{canary type}: Claude's 12\% rate (vs.\ 60\% for DeepSeek; \S\ref{sec:c4}) is consistent with training that targets boundary reasoning but cannot reach implicit gap-filling. When authorization scope violations do occur, 69.2\% involve unauthorized escalation---granting permissions beyond policy scope---with zero instances of conservative refusal across all five models ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial test), revealing a systematic permissiveness bias under boundary ambiguity.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Pooled violation rate change after type-specific disambiguation instructions (5 types, 1,256 episodes).}
\label{tab:mitigation_main}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
Type & Pooled $\Delta$pp \\
\midrule
Auth.\ scope & $-18.0$ \\
Lexical & $-8.9$ \\
Cond.\ prec. & $-8.1$ \\
Scopal & $-4.3$ \\
Incompleteness & $-3.4$ \\
\midrule
Overall & $-8.5$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Policy auditing should be \emph{type-aware}. Table~\ref{tab:mitigation_main} shows that disambiguation instructions reduce violations by 8.5 pp overall, but with stark type dependence: authorization scope benefits most ($-18.0$\,pp), while incompleteness is nearly immune ($-3.4$\,pp). This mirrors the mechanistic distinction---explicit boundary reasoning responds to corrective instructions, while implicit gap-filling does not. However, DeepSeek shows increased violations for scopal ($+2.0$\,pp) and incompleteness ($+4.0$\,pp), indicating that mitigation requires model-specific calibration (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}). A proof-of-concept classifier (Appendix~\ref{app:detection}) achieves F1\,=\,0.81 for binary detection but only 0.34 for incompleteness, compounding the challenge: the most dangerous types are least detectable. With $<$11\% agent self-awareness (\S\ref{sec:analysis}), externalized detection tools \citep{chantree_2006,ferrari_ambiguity_2019} with human review remain necessary.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around four analyses of increasing specificity: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type hierarchy (C2), the aggregate layer effect (C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 constitute our primary claims; C3 is inconclusive; C4 is exploratory but provides the model$\times$type interaction analysis.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). Of 1,497 matched pairs, the discordant ratio is 15.1:1 (544 ambiguous-only vs.\ 36 unambiguous-only violations). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity ($n = 300$, $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$\,pp, CI containing zero), and results replicate under an alternative severity threshold (Appendix~\ref{app:severity}). Clause identity explains 22.4\% of variance versus 1.0\% for model identity. A replication on two additional model families (Gemini-2.5-Flash, LLaMA-4-Scout; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}) confirms the binary effect across seven models spanning six providers ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp, both $p < 10^{-6}$).

\subsection{Type Hierarchy (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) under ambiguous policy clauses by type and model. $\Delta$pp denotes the pooled difference between ambiguous and unambiguous conditions across all five models. All per-type differences are significant ($p < 10^{-5}$, Fisher's exact test).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrrr}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & GPT-5.4 & GPT-4.1 & Claude & Qwen3 & DeepSeek & $\Delta$pp & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{speccolor}{Spec.}} & Incompleteness & 53.6 & 46.0 & 58.0 & 48.0 & 62.0 & 54.0 & +49.2 & $<10^{-37}$ \\
 & Auth.\ Scope & 41.6 & 64.0 & 46.0 & 12.0 & 26.0 & 60.0 & +36.0 & $<10^{-22}$ \\
 & Cond.\ Precedence & 36.8 & 34.0 & 36.0 & 36.0 & 40.0 & 38.0 & +30.8 & $<10^{-17}$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Ling.}} & Lexical & 42.8 & 42.0 & 34.0 & 32.0 & 52.0 & 54.0 & +35.2 & $<10^{-20}$ \\
 & Scopal & 39.4 & 42.0 & 38.0 & 32.0 & 36.7 & 48.0 & +34.2 & $<10^{-21}$ \\
 & Coreferential & 31.6 & 18.0 & 32.0 & 22.0 & 42.0 & 44.0 & +18.7 & $<10^{-6}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{41.0} & 41.0 & 40.7 & 30.3 & 43.1 & 49.7 & \textbf{+34.0} & $<10^{-105}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The six types are not interchangeable. An omnibus $\chi^2$ test on ambiguous-condition violation rates rejects the uniformity null ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $df = 5$, $p = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$; statistical power $= 0.993$ at the observed effect). TOST equivalence testing at a $\pm 15$ pp margin reveals a three-tier structure: incompleteness stands alone at the top (53.6\%), a mid-tier cluster spans four types (lexical 42.8\%, authorization scope 41.6\%, scopal 39.4\%, conditional precedence 36.8\%), and coreferential occupies the bottom (31.6\%). Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} present the full breakdown.

Sensitivity analysis across seven margins ($\pm 5$--$20$ pp; Appendix~\ref{app:tost}) confirms this structure.

Incompleteness produces the highest violation rate in four of five models (all except GPT-5.4, where authorization scope ranks first at 64\%), with rates $\geq$46\% confirmed in all seven models tested (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}). Incompleteness is structurally distinct: its unambiguous variant adds policy content rather than merely resolving ambiguity, so its 49.2 pp effect is an upper bound; a confound analysis confirms the effect remains significant after controlling for information delta (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). This pattern replicates on two additional model families (Gemini 48\%, LLaMA 52\%; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}), further suggesting that incompleteness severity reflects a genuine property of the ambiguity type rather than a dataset artifact. Coreferential, whose variants are lexically identical, provides a confound-free lower bound at 31.6\%. Excluding incompleteness, pairwise contrasts show authorization scope and lexical remain significantly above coreferential (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp, ambiguous minus unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. All types produce significant increases. A three-tier hierarchy emerges: incompleteness $\gg$ mid cluster $\gg$ coreferential.}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $p = 0.017$), but C3 is inconclusive: it does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction, and controlling for token delta reverses the direction. Cross-layer type overlap (conditional precedence 36.8\% vs.\ scopal 39.4\%) further undermines the distinction.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and ambiguity type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52 pp across models (Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), while incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%).}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure*}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), consistent with safety training that addresses boundary reasoning; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{universal vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The authorization scope pattern (Claude lowest, DeepSeek highest) is robust under both severity thresholds and within-judge analysis (Claude 12\%, Qwen3 26\%, DeepSeek 60\%, all judged by GPT-5.4).

\section{TOST Equivalence Sensitivity}
\label{app:tost}

Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} reports the TOST equivalence analysis \citep{lakens_2017} across seven margins. Three pairs involving incompleteness never reach equivalence even at $\pm 20$ pp: conditional precedence--incompleteness ($\Delta = 16.8$ pp), coreferential--incompleteness ($\Delta = 22.0$ pp), and incompleteness--scopal ($\Delta = 14.2$ pp).

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{TOST equivalence sensitivity across margins. Mid-tier = fraction of the six within-cluster pairs (lexical, auth.\ scope, scopal, cond.\ prec.) that are equivalent. Inc.\ and Cor.\ columns indicate separation from the mid-tier cluster.}
\label{tab:tost_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Margin & Equiv.\ pairs & Mid-tier & Inc.\ sep. & Cor.\ sep. \\
\midrule
$\pm 5$ pp & 0/15 & 0/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 8$ pp & 0/15 & 0/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 10$ pp & 3/15 & 3/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 12$ pp & 5/15 & 5/6 & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
$\pm 15$ pp & 8/15 & 6/6 & \checkmark & -- \\
$\pm 18$ pp & 9/15 & 6/6 & \checkmark & -- \\
$\pm 20$ pp & 12/15 & 6/6 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Mitigation Experiment}
\label{app:mitigation}

Table~\ref{tab:mitigation} reports the per-type, per-model $\Delta$pp from the disambiguation-instruction mitigation experiment (1,256 treatment episodes across five of six types; coreferential was not tested). The treatment appends a type-specific disambiguation instruction to the agent's system prompt. Control rates are the matched 5-type subset from the full study (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\begin{table*}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{Violation rate change ($\Delta$pp) after adding type-specific disambiguation instructions. Negative values indicate reduction. Baseline violation rates are shown in parentheses. Coreferential was excluded because it was not included in the mitigation experiment.}
\label{tab:mitigation}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}
\toprule
Type & GPT-5.4 & GPT-4.1 & Claude & Qwen3 & DeepSeek & Pooled \\
\midrule
Auth.\ scope & $-40.0$ (64) & $-26.0$ (46) & $0.0$ (12) & $-14.0$ (26) & $-10.0$ (60) & $-18.0$ (42) \\
Lexical & $-16.5$ (42) & $-10.0$ (34) & $-8.0$ (32) & $-8.0$ (52) & $-2.0$ (54) & $-8.9$ (43) \\
Cond.\ prec. & $-4.6$ (34) & $-10.0$ (36) & $-12.0$ (36) & $-2.0$ (40) & $-12.0$ (38) & $-8.1$ (37) \\
Scopal & $-6.7$ (42) & $-4.0$ (38) & $-10.0$ (32) & $-2.7$ (37) & $+2.0$ (48) & $-4.3$ (39) \\
Incomp. & $-2.6$ (46) & $-14.0$ (58) & $-2.0$ (48) & $-2.0$ (62) & $+4.0$ (54) & $-3.4$ (54) \\
\midrule
Overall & $-14.0$ (46) & $-12.8$ (42) & $-6.4$ (32) & $-5.8$ (43) & $-3.6$ (51) & $-8.5$ (43) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\section{Severity Threshold Comparison}
\label{app:severity}

Table~\ref{tab:severity_comparison} compares all four claims under two violation severity thresholds. The primary analysis uses moderate-or-above; the alternative includes 198 additional minor violations (6.6\% of episodes).

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{Claim-level comparison under two severity thresholds.}
\label{tab:severity_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Claim & Moderate+ & Any-violation & Concordance \\
\midrule
C1 $\Delta$ & 34.0 pp & 38.2 pp & Both $p < 10^{-50}$ \\
C2 $V$ & 0.137 & 0.169 & Both $p < 10^{-4}$ \\
C3 $\Delta$ & 6.1 pp & 7.4 pp & Both $p < 0.02$ \\
C4 $V$ & 0.102 & 0.097 & Both $p < 10^{-5}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Type ranking} & \multicolumn{2}{l}{Kendall $\tau = 0.73$, $p = 0.056$} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Ambiguity Type Detection Experiment}
\label{app:detection}

To assess whether our taxonomy supports automated identification, we conduct a proof-of-concept detection experiment using GPT-4.1 as a zero-shot classifier. Given a policy clause, the classifier determines (1)~whether it contains ambiguity and (2)~the specific type from a five-type subset of our taxonomy. Coreferential ambiguity is excluded because its variants are lexically identical to their unambiguous counterparts, making surface-level type detection inapplicable.

We evaluate on 500 policy clauses (250 ambiguous, 250 unambiguous) using stratified 5-fold cross-validation, with clause pairs kept in the same fold to prevent leakage. Table~\ref{tab:detection} reports per-type results.

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{Proof-of-concept detection classifier (GPT-4.1, zero-shot, 5-fold CV). Binary ambiguity detection: F1\,=\,0.81, FPR\,=\,4.4\%. Six-class macro F1\,=\,0.63.}
\label{tab:detection}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Type & Prec. & Recall & F1 \\
\midrule
Cond.\ precedence & 0.96 & 0.92 & 0.94 \\
Scopal & 0.91 & 0.64 & 0.75 \\
Auth.\ scope & 1.00 & 0.36 & 0.53 \\
Lexical & 0.36 & 0.42 & 0.39 \\
Incompleteness & 0.48 & 0.26 & 0.34 \\
\midrule
No ambiguity & 0.76 & 0.96 & 0.85 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Detection difficulty is inversely correlated with agent vulnerability: incompleteness, the most dangerous type (53.6\% violation rate, \S\ref{sec:results}), is the hardest to detect (F1\,=\,0.34), while conditional precedence (36.8\% violation rate) is the easiest (F1\,=\,0.94). This asymmetry arises because types with overt linguistic signals (e.g., conditional structures) are readily detectable but only moderately dangerous, whereas missing information leaves no surface trace for a classifier to exploit.

The primary confusion pathway is authorization scope $\to$ lexical (48\% of authorization scope clauses): authorization boundary clauses frequently contain polysemous terms, causing the classifier to attribute the ambiguity to word-level rather than permission-level underspecification.

These results position automated detection as a viable first-pass screening tool (binary F1\,=\,0.81) but an unreliable type classifier, particularly for the highest-risk types. The classifier was evaluated on synthetic clauses derived from $\tau^2$-bench; generalization to naturally occurring policy documents requires further validation.

\section{Replication on Additional Model Families}
\label{app:model_expansion}

To test cross-architecture generalization beyond the five main-study models, we replicate the core analyses on two additional model families: Gemini-2.5-Flash (Google) and LLaMA-4-Scout (Meta), each with 250 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $= 500$ episodes judged by GPT-5.4. These models represent two additional providers not in the original study. Tables~\ref{tab:expansion_overall} and~\ref{tab:expansion_pertype} report the results.

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{Replication results on two additional model families. C2 rank $\rho$ is computed against the original five-model pooled type ranking.}
\label{tab:expansion_overall}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrl}
\toprule
Model & Amb.\ (\%) & Unamb.\ (\%) & $\Delta$pp & $p$ & C2 $\rho$ \\
\midrule
Gemini-2.5-Flash & 37.6 & 4.0 & +33.6 & $<10^{-21}$ & 0.60 \\
LLaMA-4-Scout & 37.6 & 17.6 & +20.0 & $<10^{-6}$ & 0.90$^*$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{Per-type ambiguous violation rates (\%) for replication models vs.\ original five-model pooled rates.}
\label{tab:expansion_pertype}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Type & Gemini & LLaMA & Orig.\ pooled \\
\midrule
Incompleteness & 48 & 52 & 53.6 \\
Lexical & 50 & 38 & 42.8 \\
Auth.\ scope & 22 & 40 & 41.6 \\
Scopal & 34 & 30 & 39.4 \\
Cond.\ prec. & 34 & 28 & 36.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{C1 replication.} Both models show significant binary ambiguity effects (Gemini $\Delta = +33.6$\,pp, OR $= 14.5$, $p < 10^{-21}$; LLaMA $\Delta = +20.0$\,pp, OR $= 2.8$, $p < 10^{-6}$), extending C1 to seven models across six providers. Gemini's effect size falls within the original five-model range (+25.7 to +39.0\,pp). LLaMA's smaller effect and lower OR are attributable to an elevated unambiguous baseline (17.6\% vs.\ $<$10\% for all other models), suggesting weaker baseline instruction following that compresses the ambiguity-specific delta.

\paragraph{C2 replication.} Both models' type rankings correlate positively with the original pooled ranking. LLaMA shows a strong and significant correlation ($\rho = 0.90$, $p = 0.037$), with incompleteness as the top type and the bottom three types matching the reference order exactly. Gemini shows a moderate positive correlation ($\rho = 0.60$, $p = 0.285$), with the top two types (lexical and incompleteness) swapped relative to the reference. Incompleteness remains $\geq$46\% in all seven models (range: 46--62\%), confirming its status as a universal vulnerability independent of architecture or provider. $^*$Significant at $\alpha = 0.05$.

\section{Incompleteness Confound Analysis}
\label{app:confound}

Incompleteness is structurally distinct from the other five types: its unambiguous variant necessarily adds policy content (specifying what was previously missing) rather than merely resolving an existing ambiguity. This raises a confound: the 49.2 pp effect may partly reflect the information addition rather than ambiguity resolution per se.

A logistic regression controlling for per-clause information delta (token count difference between ambiguous and unambiguous variants) confirms that incompleteness remains individually significant ($p = 0.022$, coefficient change $< 8\%$ relative to the uncontrolled model). Coreferential ambiguity, whose variants are lexically identical (information delta $= 0$), provides a confound-free lower bound: its 31.6\% violation rate and 18.7 pp effect are entirely attributable to semantic ambiguity.

Excluding incompleteness, the remaining five types show a non-significant omnibus difference ($\chi^2$, $p = 0.079$), but pairwise contrasts with Holm--Bonferroni correction reveal a two-tier pattern: authorization scope ($p_{\text{adj}} = 0.039$) and lexical ($p_{\text{adj}} = 0.025$) remain significantly above coreferential, while the four remaining pairs are equivalent at the $\pm 15$ pp margin.

